<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- 2026-07-23: see android/app/src/canary/AndroidManifest.xml for why
         this exists. Production's channel is "production" per eas.json,
         distinct from canary/preview's "preview".

         2026-08-05: the meta-data name below was "EXPO_UPDATE_REQUEST_HEADERS"
         — that key is NEVER read by the installed expo-updates ~29.0.18 native
         module. Traced through UpdatesConfiguration.kt's
         getOriginalEmbeddedRequestHeaders(): for a normal (non-dev-client)
         launch, overrideMap is null (see UpdatesController.kt, which always
         constructs `UpdatesConfiguration(context, null)`), so it falls
         through to a manifest meta-data lookup under the literal string
         "expo.modules.updates.UPDATES_CONFIGURATION_REQUEST_HEADERS_KEY" —
         apparently an upstream bug where the Kotlin constant's own NAME got
         used as the lookup string instead of a real short key. Whatever the
         cause, that's what this SDK version actually reads, confirmed by
         reading the source directly (not from docs). With the old key name,
         requestHeaders silently resolved to {} and expo-channel-name was
         never sent, so every update check 400'd with
         '"channel-name": Required' and OTA was completely broken on this
         binary. Fixed by using the (odd but verified-correct) key name
         below. Revisit if/when expo-updates ships a version that fixes this
         upstream — check whether the old proper-looking key name works
         again before removing this one. -->
    <application>
        <meta-data android:name="expo.modules.updates.UPDATES_CONFIGURATION_REQUEST_HEADERS_KEY" android:value="{&quot;expo-channel-name&quot;:&quot;production&quot;}"/>
    </application>
</manifest>
